 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Not yet.** Inflation has picked back up over the past year and is above the RBA's 2–3% target band, though it isn't running away.\n\n- **Headline CPI:** 4.0% year-on-year in August 2026, up from 1.9% in June 2025. It peaked at 4.6% in March 2026 and has eased somewhat since.\n- **Trimmed mean (the RBA's preferred underlying measure):** 3.6% in August 2026, up from 2.8% in June 2025. This is the better guide to persistent inflation, and it has risen steadily for over a year.\n- **Electricity:** up 13.2% year-on-year in August 2026, after a sharp fall in late 2024. Electricity prices are a large part of the headline increase.\n- **Rents:** 3.6% in August 2026, down from 7.1% in June 2024. This is the one clearly cooling component.\n\nThe RBA has responded by lifting the cash rate from 3.60% (January 2026) to 4.35% by June 2026. It was 4.36% as a September 2026 monthly average, so policy is tighter. The latest CPI data run to August 2026, and the September quarter figures are not yet in.",
